{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>See, I set before you this day blessing and curse.</b> 'See' is as one speaking to an individual; 'before you' is as one speaking to the multitudes — because our Sages, blessed be their memory, said (Kiddushin 40b): a person should always regard himself as though the entire world were half merits and half guilty — if he performs one commandment, he tips himself and the entire world toward the side of merit. Therefore He said to each individual 'See,' that he should see with the eye of his intellect that all his deeds return 'before you,' to all of you. And He recalled this matter in this pericope, which speaks of the matter of Mount Gerizim and Mount Ebal, for there all Israel became guarantors for one another, and from the aspect of surety the multitudes are held accountable for the sin of the individual. And there is an allusion resembling this, in what the Yalkut (§650) brings at the beginning of this pericope: a parable of one who sat at a crossroads, and before him were two paths, etc. It began with roads trodden by the multitudes, and concluded with paths trodden by individuals — only he set forth this parable upon a verse that begins with the individual and concludes with the multitudes, for the reason that was explained. And He employed the language of 'this day' even though there is a fixed time for the acceptance of the oath, 'for HaShem your Elohim is bringing you into the land' — in any event He employed the language of 'this day' to say that from this day until that time you have leisure to deliberate, so that they should not say 'we were coerced' — in the manner of every oath, that they give time lest the man change his mind; so He gave them time so that they should not say, 'He overwhelmed us and confounded us,' and had they had leisure to deliberate they would not have done so." "Another matter: with the word 'this day' He alluded to the daily sphere, and He juxtaposed it to blessing and curse to tell you: just as the sun, which is one, produces two opposites — it softens the wax and hardens the egg, blackens the face of the washer and whitens the garment — and all these changes do not come from the side of the sun but only from the side of the recipients, so too the blessings and the curses that come from Him, blessed be He, do not require change in Him, blessed be He, as it is written (Malachi 3:6), ״אֲנִי ה׳ לֹא שָׁנִיתִי״ — rather the change comes from the side of the recipients, and from the mouth of the Most High the evil and the good do not go forth (Lamentations 3:38), as is found in the midrash that the Akeidah brought in this pericope — and I say that He fixed it with the word 'this day.'" "Another matter: because we find among our Sages, blessed be their memory, regarding the blessing that they said (Nedarim 8b): there is no Gehinom for the future to come, but the Holy One, blessed be He, takes the sun out of its sheath, etc., as it is said (Malachi 3:19), ״וְלִהַט אוֹתָם הַיּוֹם הַבָּא״ — and 'the day' is the sun; but concerning the righteous it is written (ibid. 3:20), ״וְזָרְחָה לָכֶם יִרְאֵי שְׁמִי שֶׁמֶשׁ צְדָקָה וּמַרְפֵּא בִּכְנָפֶיהָ״. Therefore it says 'this day blessing and curse' — from the daily sphere blessing and curse shall be drawn, for in this world there is no reward for a commandment. And therefore He gave a sign: ״אַחֲרֵי דֶּרֶךְ מְבוֹא הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ״ etc. In this verse He gave signs and boundary-markers more than one who sells his field — and 'the way of the sun's setting' — what do I need it for? Rather, He comes to hint that the essence of the receiving of reward and punishment is by way of the sun's setting."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The Yalkut citation תתעה is rendered as §650. The reading הממם וערבבם ('overwhelmed/confounded and confused') is translated hyper-literally; the phrase is textually unusual and may reflect a variant or corruption."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:12:18.996621664Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}